From: dasher@netcom.com (D. Anton Sherwood)
Subject: nominalism is what the other says

A long time ago, in fact on August 8, James Donald wrote:
> For example if nominalism was correct, then what the
> classical Greeks said would be largely meaningless to us,
> for example it is unlikely that they would use a word
> corresponding to "liberty" in a sense similar to that which
> we use. This prediction is obviously false.	Liberty and
> similar concepts are plainly true universals, thus plainly
> universals exist.

Plainly.  Except that when Westerners tried to promulgate liberalism in
China, they had to invent a word for liberty.

Among the memes we inherit from Greece are many of our words (directly or
calqued through Latin).  Any Darwinist should recognize that this does NOT
mean the lexicon is universal or "natural".

"He is a barbarian, and thinks the customs of his island to be the laws of
nature."  (Bernard Shaw, CAESAR AND CLEOPATRA, likely misquoted)

> Thus all theories based on nominalism, for example Marxism
> and Utilitarianism, must be false, Marxism because of its
> nominalist definition of value, utilitarianism because of
> its nominalist definition of the good.

Why shouldn't a Marxist say *our* definition of value is nominalist?  After
all we do say one person's measurement of value is as good as another's,
while Marxists have an objective definition of something that they call
"value" (though "cost" is a better word for it).

You might call me an evolutionary or market nominalist:  Particular concepts
(sets) have no privileges *a priori* but must compete for recognition by
virtue of their descriptive usefulness.  Green is more useful than grue
because leaves are (as far as we know:) green, not grue.

