Subject: phys: gravitational anti-energy

Humor me just a little bit longer.

D. Anton Sherwood wrote on August 7:
> Between two masses there is negative gravitational potential energy
> (-Gmm/d).  Energy is equivalent to mass, so this negative energy has
> negative gravity.  Where is it?  That is, somewhere in the system there
> should be a locus of (very weak!) repulsion; is it in the masses, in a
> cloud between them, in their joint center of mass, or what?


Ray Cromwell replied on August 8:
>   Intuitively, I would guess that gravity doesn't gravitate because that
> would lead to a runaway affect.

No harm done -- the feedback is negative (and slight).

>   Also, figuring out how energy/mass gravitate is a bit more difficult
> than applying Newton's law of gravitation. (it takes General Relativity)

That doesn't seem to change the principle of my question.

>   People tend to go overboard when applying relativity's ideas of mass
> and energy. A common mistake is to ask "if mass increases as you go
> faster, why wouldn't a very fast spaceship turn into a black hole?"

Donald Moffitt used that rather silly premise in "The Mechanical Sky"
(Crescent in the Sky / A Gathering of Stars).


Dave Krieger wrote on August 9:
> This is a rather clever paradox, Anton,

Paradox?

> but fundamentally it just comes down to the reification of a bookkeeping
> trick (or, more charitably, a mathematical abstraction).

... and goes on to imply that kinetic energy isn't real, either, which was
news to me.  Are potential and kinetic energy just a fiction that physicists
use to keep the books square?

I agree there's nothing real about defining zero to be the grav.pot.energy
at infinite separation, but that's not the important thing anyway, as I see
it.  If we set zero lower (so that it's possible for two masses to have
positive GPE), we get an infinite universe permeated by pseudo-mass, most
of which cancels out, leaving pockets of negative pseudo-mass.

