From: tburns@gmuvax.gmu.edu (T. David Burns)
Subject: AIT:Socialist computation

At  1:12 PM 9/1/93 +0000, Ray wrote:
><statist economist mode on>
>  It is true that a central planner could not accurately model the
>outcomes of individuals and small organizations. 

This is a different question. Socialists don't need such a model, they need
a method of coordinating production without prices. While having such a
model might suffice, it isn't necessary.

>Analagous to thermodynamics then, could not an economist model
>macroscopic variables of an economy? In the same way a physicist can
>manipulate temperature by changing volume and pressure, could not an
>economist change the GNP by manipulating the money supply, taxes, and
>subsidies?  
><statist economist mode off>

Complex systems stuff like far-from-equilibrium thermodynamics, or solid
state physics might hold better analogies, since they depend more on local
interaction between the agents/particles rather than just stochastic
motion. I don't know much about these fields, but spin glasses and
convection cells sound more like an economy than an ideal gas does. You can
control convection cells with temperature, etc. within limits, but you
can't make it sit up and beg.

Hayek talked about pattern prediction. Certainly economists would like to
be able to say *something* about what causes changes in the macroeconomy. I
think this level of competence is achievable, though I'm not confident that
we've yet achieved it. (Bush/Clinton sure knew/knows how to get us out of a
recession, huh? ;) My intuition says that the kind of knobs politicians
prefer (manipulate $ supply, interest rates, taxes, subsidies) don't
maintain a simple cause/effect relations, or else we'd have it figured out
by now. 

