Subject: Heinlein's predictions

Reading the texts of Heinlein's worldcon speechs was interesting. (They're
in the Reqieum collection)

He goes on to describe a scenario in which the Soviets overthrow their
Government out of a desire for more and better consumer goods and greater
freedoms. Pretty much what happened.

He then says that anyone who believes inthis scenario is a fool. Because
the Soviets are true believers, who will not be seduced by sirloin steaks--

He predicted that the US would be overun, and that the Soviet's would rule
the Earth forever, because unlike most empires, the Soviets would have no
outside force to topple them. No barbarian hordes.

For Heinlein, and American conservatives, the Soviets represented a
complete, *ahistoric* evil--you couldn't compare them to any other
empire--they were qualitatively different. The fear they engendered was
hysterical, irrational.

And Heinlein, perhaps my favorite writer, was as irrational as anyone in
this area.

Jay



